WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.330
For many Managed Apps, one app is all you need.

00:00:03.820 --> 00:00:07.830
A maker builds it, shares it with a small team, and keeps improving it.

00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:10.830
No extra source control, no extra environments.

00:00:12.109 --> 00:00:13.359
But some apps take off.

00:00:14.049 --> 00:00:19.059
Hundreds, then thousands of people rely on them, and every change now carries real risk.

00:00:19.970 --> 00:00:21.220
That's when the app graduates.

00:00:21.850 --> 00:00:24.420
The team registers three apps from the same code:

00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:28.880
dev, test and prod, each pinned to its own governed environment.

00:00:29.837 --> 00:00:35.087
The code moves into the team's own GitHub repository, with a branch for each stage.

00:00:35.657 --> 00:00:39.687
Every change starts on a feature branch, and gets reviewed in a pull request.

00:00:40.706 --> 00:00:42.156
Promoting is just a merge:

00:00:42.706 --> 00:00:44.876
dev into test, then test into prod.

00:00:45.486 --> 00:00:50.136
Each deploy builds from its branch, so every stage stays bound to its own environment.

00:00:51.170 --> 00:00:54.180
Testers try changes before users ever see them.

00:00:54.490 --> 00:00:58.220
And if a release goes wrong, prod rolls back to the last good commit.

00:00:58.910 --> 00:01:00.160
Small apps stay simple.

00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:01.920
Critical apps get the rigor.
